    fields:  # 95bb
      statement: "A taint finding marked walk_blocked_by=sink_before_source has no demonstrated route, and on the current cohort both verdicts that rest on such a row alone are false; the tool reports them and lets them move a verdict from inconclusive to violated."  # 95bb
      root_cause: "ADR-0017 s3a is confirm-only, so walk_blocked_by is recorded and never acted on. That was measured when the shape was 0.6% of rows; a primitive that is both a source and a sink raises it to 25% of the rows it adds, and nothing distinguishes a loop (where the pair can be real) from straight-line code (where it cannot)."  # 95bb
      description: "FOUND 2026-08-31 measuring INV-lozat, and found because the fix that closes one\ngap makes the next one countable rather than by looking for it.\n\nTHE SHAPE. hypergumbo's dataflow walk records `walk_blocked_by:\nsink_before_source` when every sink call site in a finding is reached BEFORE the\nsource call in the same function. The finding is still reported: ADR-0017 s3a is\nconfirm-only and taint.py says so where the field is defined -- \"NOTHING IS\nACTED ON ... No verdict moves because of this field.\" That is a deliberate,\nratified position and this item does NOT propose overturning it by default: a\nloop makes a textually-earlier sink genuinely reachable from a later source, so\nthe marker is \"the walk could not run\", not \"the flow is impossible\".\n\nWHAT MAKES IT ACTIONABLE NOW IS THE RATE AND WHAT SITS BEHIND IT. Measured over\nmeasurement 0006's 16-repository cohort, every arm cold, using the tool's own\nfield:\n\n    dev before INV-lozat        4 of 692 rows    0.6%\n    INV-lozat, rows only       18 of  51 added  35.3%\n    INV-lozat as shipped       10 of  40 added  25.0%   (corpus rate 1.9%)\n\nINV-lozat's `_source_and_sink_are_one_call` answers the case where the source\ncall and the sink call are ONE call. It does not answer the case where a\nDIFFERENT sink call textually precedes the source in the same function, which\nis the residual 25%.\n\nTWO INSTANCES, READ BACK AGAINST SOURCE RATHER THAN ASSERTED:\n\n  gocryptfs internal/fido2/fido2.go:38-70  callFidoCommand\n      `io.WriteString(in, s+\"\\n\")` writes the CHILD'S STDIN; `cmd.Output()`\n      reads its stdout four lines later. \"Subprocess output reaches a file\n      write\" is false, and this SINGLE row is the whole of gocryptfs's\n      inconclusive -> violated move on untrusted-input-no-host-fs.\n\n  plausible tracker/compiler/analyze-sizes.js:283-297  readFileSize\n      Two `execSync` calls (gzip, then brotli). The edge records two call\n      lines, so INV-lozat's gate ABSTAINS by design -- at two sites the pair\n      CAN be real. Here it is not: the gzip result feeds `.length`, not\n      brotli's argument. This single row is the whole of plausible's\n      inconclusive -> violated move.\n\nSo on this cohort, BOTH verdict moves that survive INV-lozat's gate rest on a\nrow that is false. That is what makes this worth pricing rather than noting.\n\nWHAT WOULD HAVE TO BE ESTABLISHED BEFORE ANYTHING CHANGES, because this is the\nREMOVAL direction and s3a is ratified:\n  1. How often is a `sink_before_source` finding actually true? The loop case is\n     the whole defence and nobody has counted it. A census of the 14 rows on the\n     current corpus, read against source, is the cheapest possible answer.\n  2. Can the walk distinguish \"sink precedes source, no loop encloses either\"\n     from \"sink precedes source inside a loop\"? The CFG has back-edges; whether\n     the DDG plumbing exposes them at this point is unknown and is the real\n     feasibility question.\n  3. If (1) says the true rate is not ~0, the answer is NOT to remove -- it is\n     to stop these rows from moving a VERDICT while still reporting them, which\n     is a third thing s3a does not currently express.\nDo not skip (1). \"Presence of a finding after a change is not evidence the\nfinding is correct\" cost a published retraction once already."  # 95bb
- op: discuss  # ceac
  at: '2026-09-01T00:08:52Z'  # ceac
  by: agent  # ceac
  actor: jgstern_agent  # ceac
  clock: 2  # ceac
  nonce: ceac  # ceac
  message: "STEP 1 DONE — THE CENSUS. Artifact:\n~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/muhij_sink_before_source_census_08312026/ (README first).\nCohort = measurement 0006's 16 repos on current dev e66ab343a3, every repo cold\n(each run asserts its own \"No cached results found\"), evidence lists uncapped.\nPopulation is IDENTICAL to arm C: 14 rows (beads 9, cilium 2, gocryptfs 3), all\ncarrying verdict violated. Zero added, zero removed — checked by re-running the\nwhole cohort rather than assumed from \"WI-zovuz only touched bash\".\n\n(1) HOW OFTEN IS SUCH A FINDING TRUE? 1 of 14 (7.1%). All 14 read back against\nrepository source; per-row verdicts and reasoning in readback.json.\n\n(2) THE LOOP DEFENCE IS REFUTED — 0 of 14. This item's premise was that a loop\nis what makes a textually-earlier sink genuinely reachable, and that \"the loop\ncase is the whole defence\". No row has a loop enclosing both calls. Loops appear\nin four rows and enclose neither call (2, 4, 9, 10) or enclose only the SINK and\nterminate before the source (14: `for _, s := range stdin` closes before\nin.Close()).\n\nTHE REAL DEFENCE IS STRUCTURAL, NOT STATISTICAL: A SINK CALL SITE THAT IS A\nCONSTRUCTOR WHOSE EFFECT IS DEFERRED. gocryptfs daemonize.go:29-62 forkChild:\n\n    c := exec.Command(name, newArgs...)   // L46  <- the catalogued sink\n    c.Stdin  = os.Stdin                   // L49  <- the source\n    err = c.Start()                       // L51  <- where the crossing happens\n\nexec.Command does not launch anything; it builds a *exec.Cmd. go.yaml rows it\nunder `subprocess` because that is where tainted ARGUMENTS enter, and rows\nCmd.Run/Cmd.Start separately. So `sink_line < source_line` does not imply \"the\ncrossing precedes the source\" — here the crossing is two lines AFTER it.\n\nThis is ADR-0049's own vocabulary turned on the ordering test, and the tree\nalready says it twice: go.yaml's ipc_recv note (\"a handle is a crossing that has\nnot happened yet, the ADR-0049 deferred shape\") and go.yaml's net_listen note\n(\"in one of them the sink path begins at a call that runs BEFORE the\nListenAndServe statement\"). The load-bearing point does NOT depend on ruling\nrow 12's finding interesting — a self re-exec inheriting stdin is a real but\nbenign data path. Even a reader who calls it uninteresting must accept that the\nORDERING signal is wrong there, so a remedy acting on it removes a flow on a\nfalse premise.\n\n(3) Therefore not removal, which is what this item already predicted. Two\nfindings constrain HOW remedy (3) can be built.\n\nFINDING A — THE MARKER DOES NOT DESCRIBE THE ROW IT IS PRINTED ON.\ncollapse_unadjudicated_flows groups on (taint_label, source_symbol, sink_zone,\nsanitized, source_boundary, analysis_method). walk_blocked_by is NOT in that key\nand is NOT unioned — the group inherits grp[0]'s. And s3a computes\nsink_before_source only when sink_node == source_fn, so a member whose sink sits\nin another function gets cross_function and can never carry the marker.\n\nMeasured by instrumenting the pre-collapse groups on beads, and the instrument\nRECONCILES EXACTLY with published output (that is why its numbers are usable):\n9 production claim-matching groups == the 9 published beads rows; 28 member\npairs == the armC collapsed_flow_count sum; 10 marker-carrying members == what\nan independent structural count predicts.\n\n    28 member pairs under the 9 published beads rows\n    10 (35.7%) actually carry the marker; 18 are cross_function\n\n    Over all 145 sink_before_source groups (test-scoped included):\n    462 member pairs — 248 (53.7%) carry it, 214 (46.3%) do not\n        174 cross_function · 25 source_not_tracked · 15 blocked_by EMPTY\n    15 members had the walk actually RUN inside a group labelled \"the walk\n    never ran because the sink precedes the source\".\n    70 of 145 groups (48.3%) contain at least one disagreeing member.\n\nCONSEQUENCE: a verdict-suppression rule keyed on the ROW's walk_blocked_by would\nact on a fact true of about one member in three. The marker must first be\nrecomputed as a property of the GROUP (all members), not the representative.\n\nFINDING B — THE call_lines CAP'S \"CONSERVATIVE\" GUARANTEE EXPIRES UNDER (3).\nir.py truncates meta[\"call_lines\"] to the LOWEST _CALL_LINES_CAP (50) lines and\nstates truncation \"is CONSERVATIVE for every consumer ... knowing fewer call\nsites can only narrow what a dataflow walk adjudicates, never broaden it\". That\nholds while s3a is confirm-only: dropping high-numbered sites can only cost a\nconfirmation. It STOPS holding the moment sink_before_source gates a verdict —\na callee invoked at >50 distinct lines keeps the lowest 50, and if the source\nsits above all of them, sink_after_source is computed False on truncated data\nand a real later call site is suppressed unseen. Remedy (3) must exempt\ntruncated edges or record the truncation.\n\nSTATUS STAYS violated. Nothing is fixed; this is the measurement the item made a\nprecondition. Step 2 as originally written (can the walk see loop back-edges?)\nis now MUCH lower value, because loops are 0 of 14. The question that replaced\nit: can the ordering test anchor on the EFFECT call rather than the constructor\n— which is WI-kanor's family (handle-returning launches) seen from the taint\nside."  # ceac
